//! LiberSystem display-device package - the one wire between DisplayService and a display driver.
//!
//! WHY IT IS TYPED. This boundary was seven byte strings: `FB`, `FBNEW`, `RESIZE`, `FLUSH`,
//! `PRESENT`, `OK` and `ERR`, with the framebuffer description memcpy'd out of a message at an offset
//! and four little-endian `u32`s decoded by hand on the other side. Every other service boundary in
//! this tree is LSIDL with typed capabilities, and this one is the boundary that hands over a MAPPED
//! DMA BACKING - the one where a length read at the wrong offset is a mapping of the wrong size.
//!
//! AND WHY ITS LIFECYCLE IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. Eight questions decide whether
//! two implementations of this wire agree, and none of them was answered anywhere:
//!
//!   WHO OWNS THE BACKING AND FOR HOW LONG. The DRIVER owns it. It creates the DMA object, keeps its
//!   own reference, and hands DisplayService a duplicate that is valid until the backing is REPLACED
//!   or the connection is lost. DisplayService unmaps and closes its handle on either, and a handle
//!   outliving its generation is a mapping of memory the driver may have given back.
//!
//!   HOW A BACKING BINDS TO A GENERATION. Every `scanout` carries a generation, and the generation
//!   changes whenever the BACKING changes - a resize that reallocates, a driver restart, a mode set.
//!   A present names the generation it was drawn for.
//!
//!   WHAT A RESIZE DOES. A resize that fits inside the existing backing changes the VISIBLE extent
//!   and nothing else, and the generation does not move: the same pixels are still there. A resize
//!   that needs more than the backing holds delivers a NEW backing with a NEW generation, and the old
//!   one is released by the service as soon as it has mapped the new one.
//!
//!   WHAT PRESENT COMPLETION MEANS. It means the DRIVER ACKNOWLEDGED THE COMMAND - the transfer and
//!   the flush were issued and answered by the device. It does NOT mean a scanout was observed: this
//!   backend has no vblank, no page-flip timing and no evidence of physical presentation, and
//!   reporting a command acknowledgement as a presentation timestamp would be reporting a fact
//!   nobody has.
//!
//!   WHAT DRIVER DEATH DOES TO IN-FLIGHT PRESENTS. The call fails. A present is synchronous on this
//!   wire, so there is exactly one in flight per connection and its failure is the service's answer;
//!   there is no queue to drain and no completion to deliver late.
//!
//!   HOW DISPLAYSERVICE FINDS A NEW BACKING AFTER A DRIVER RESTART. Through the provider catalogue,
//!   which is where it found the first one: it opens the replacement publication and calls `scanout`
//!   on it. This interface has no reconnect of its own, because a connection to a dead driver cannot
//!   carry one.
//!
//!   THAT THE EVENT STREAM IS BOUNDED. `events` is a bounded channel like every other stream in this
//!   tree. A driver that produces events faster than the service consumes them loses the OLDEST, and
//!   that is safe for exactly these events: a resize and a replacement are both idempotent snapshots
//!   of the current state, so the newest one is the true one.
//!
//!   AND THAT A BACKING FROM AN OLD GENERATION CAN NEVER BE PRESENTED INTO A NEW ONE. A present
//!   naming a generation that is not the current one is refused with `stale`, not clamped, not
//!   drawn, and not silently accepted - it is a frame drawn against pixels that no longer exist.

package liber:display-device@1;

use liber:base@1.{error};
use liber:graphics@1.{damage-region, extent-2d, image-layout};

/// The scanout backing a driver hands over: where the pixels are, what they are, how much of them is
/// visible, and which generation they belong to.
record scanout {
	/// The DMA backing, as a handle the service maps. The driver keeps its own reference; this one is
	/// valid until the backing is replaced or the connection is lost.
	backing:    buffer,
	/// What the backing's pixels are. The extent here is the ALLOCATED one, which a driver may make
	/// larger than the display so a later resize needs no new backing.
	layout:     image-layout,
	/// How much of the backing the display actually shows. Never larger than the layout's extent.
	visible:    extent-2d,
	/// Which generation these pixels belong to. It changes whenever the BACKING changes.
	generation: u32,
}

/// What a driver reports between calls.
variant device-event {
	/// The display size changed INSIDE the existing backing. The generation has not moved and the
	/// pixels are the same pixels.
	resized(extent-2d),
	/// The backing was replaced: a new object, a new generation, and the old handle is the service's
	/// to release once it has mapped this one.
	replaced(scanout),
	/// The monitor description changed or became unavailable. Query `monitor` again; this notification
	/// carries no framebuffer and does not change the backing generation.
	monitor-changed,
}

/// The display device, as DisplayService drives it.
interface display-device {
	/// The current scanout. Called once at adoption and again after a rebind; a driver that cannot
	/// supply one answers `unavailable` rather than an empty backing.
	@op(1) scanout: func() -> result<scanout, error>;
	/// Transfer the damaged pixels and flush them to the device, answering when the device has
	/// acknowledged BOTH. `generation` is the generation the frame was drawn for: a present naming a
	/// generation that is no longer current is refused rather than drawn.
	@op(2) present: func(generation: u32, damage: damage-region) -> result<unit, error>;
	/// The bounded event stream: resizes and replacements.
	@op(3) events: func() -> stream<device-event>;
	/// The bound output's EDID base-block modes and physical size. Unsupported means the transport
	/// offers no EDID; unavailable means it supplied no valid description. Neither prevents scanout.
	@op(4) monitor: func() -> result<monitor-description, error>;
}

/// THE PCI FUNCTION A BACKLIGHT BELONGS TO, as the bus enumerates it - the adapter an ACPI video output sits under, or
/// a native driver's own function.
record backlight-function {
	bus:  u32,
	dev:  u32,
	func: u32,
}

/// A MONITOR'S IDENTITY, from its EDID base block: the manufacturer id, the product code and the serial number.
record monitor-identity {
	manufacturer: u16,
	product:      u16,
	serial:       u32,
}

/// One advertised base-block timing. This is discovery, not a promise that a controller can set it.
record monitor-mode {
	size:               extent-2d,
	refresh-millihertz: u32,
	interlaced:         bool,
}

record monitor-description {
	identity:         monitor-identity,
	/// Absent for an aspect ratio or unspecified size; neither is a physical measurement.
	physical-size-mm: option<extent-2d>,
	/// At most 29 modes: four detailed, seventeen established, eight standard timings, deduplicated.
	@bound(29) modes:  list<monitor-mode>,
	/// Index of the preferred detailed timing, when the EDID declares one.
	preferred:        option<u32>,
}

/// WHAT A BACKLIGHT BELONGS TO, as its driver reads it and never derives: the ACPI driver copies its adapter's function
/// from its claim's identity, the USB one reads the monitor's EDID from the device.
variant backlight-target {
	function(backlight-function),
	monitor(monitor-identity),
	none,
}

/// WHO DRIVES THE LEVEL: the firmware (ACPI's video extension), a USB monitor's control page, or a graphics driver's
/// own registers.
enum backlight-source {
	firmware,
	usb-monitor,
	native,
}

/// The levels a backlight takes: a discrete list, ascending and without duplicates, or a range.
variant backlight-scale {
	levels(list<u32>),
	range(backlight-range),
}

record backlight-range {
	minimum: u32,
	maximum: u32,
}

/// A BACKLIGHT DESCRIBES ITSELF: who drives it, the STABLE KEY a stored level is filed under - one that survives a
/// reboot (`acpi:` and the output node's path; `usb:` and vendor:product:serial, or vendor:product and the port path) -
/// its levels, the firmware's defaults on AC and on battery where it has them, and what it belongs to.
record backlight-description {
	source:          backlight-source,
	key:             string,
	scale:           backlight-scale,
	ac-default:      option<u32>,
	battery-default: option<u32>,
	target:          backlight-target,
}

/// A FIRMWARE HOTKEY, as the firmware notified it: one step up or down, cycle, or to zero.
enum backlight-hotkey {
	up,
	down,
	cycle,
	zero,
}

/// What a backlight reports between calls: its level changed by the firmware or the device itself (a monitor's own
/// buttons), or a hotkey the firmware raised and did not act on.
variant backlight-event {
	level(u32),
	hotkey(backlight-hotkey),
}

/// THE BACKLIGHT, as DisplayService drives it - on its own provider kind, beside the display wire, because display
/// brightness is its one consumer.
interface backlight {
	@op(1) describe: func() -> result<backlight-description, error>;
	/// The level now; `unavailable` where the device cannot say (no `_BQC`, nothing set yet).
	@op(2) get: func() -> result<u32, error>;
	/// Set a level from the scale, answering the level set.
	@op(3) set: func(level: u32) -> result<u32, error>;
	@op(4) events: func() -> stream<backlight-event>;
	/// ACPI output _ADR, when available; used only to choose among firmware outputs of one adapter.
	/// Additive optional metadata: consumers of older providers fall back to namespace ordering.
	@op(5) firmware-display-id: func() -> result<option<u32>, error>;
}

/// One point of a light sensor's response curve, as ACPI's `_ALR` gives it: the display adjustment in percent of
/// normal, at an illuminance in lux.
record light-response {
	adjustment:  u32,
	illuminance: u32,
}

record ambient-light-description {
	/// The firmware's response curve, ascending by illuminance, or empty where the sensor has none.
	curve: list<light-response>,
}

/// One reading, in thousandths of a lux.
record illuminance {
	milli-lux: u64,
}

/// AN AMBIENT-LIGHT SENSOR, as the brightness policy reads it. The stream's first item is the reading now, so a
/// consumer never waits for a change to learn the light.
interface ambient-light {
	@op(1) describe: func() -> result<ambient-light-description, error>;
	@op(2) events: func() -> stream<illuminance>;
}
